ish enormous scale. A stag can expand through antlers. A jellyfish can descend through long placements. A big cat can move across the thigh. A raven can use shoulder and chest. Do not put every animal inside a rectangular scene. Allow environment and animal to interlock. Use: Snow. Water. Fog. Dust. Rain. Grass. Forest. Rock. Desert. Ocean. Swamp. Night. Fire. Wind. Light. Shadow. Environment should reveal something about the animal. It should not simply become decorative background. REGIONAL WILDLIFE MATTERS. People may love animals because those animals belong to where they are from. Do not assume every animal requires universal symbolism. A coyote can matter because someone grew up hearing coyotes. A pelican can matter because someone lives on the coast. An alligator can matter because it belongs to someones landscape. A whale can matter because someone sees them migrate. The animal does not need a dictionary meaning to deserve a tattoo. DO NOT AUTOMATICALLY ASSIGN CANNED SYMBOLISM. Lion does not automatically mean courage. Wolf does not automatically mean loyalty. Owl does not automatically mean wisdom. Snake does not automatically mean rebirth. If symbolism is important, let the customer define it or explain genuine cultural associations accurately. Nature can become dark without becoming Horror. Explore: Predation. Death. Decay. Shedding. Skeletons. Migration. Survival. Carcasses. Scavengers. Deep water. Darkness. Injury. Transformation. Weather. Extinction. Life cycles. Keep the animal world central. Do not add skulls and demons simply to make an animal tattoo darker. NATURE ALREADY CONTAINS DARKNESS. Surrealism is welcome when the animal remains the hero. A whale can contain a storm. A stags antlers can become forest. A leopards spots can become stars. A zebras stripes can leave its body. A birds wings can contain night. A rhinoceros can become landscape. A manta ray can pass over an impossible city. A snake can become smoke. But do not bury the animal benea